"Take a deep breath" is common advice, but it's not quite what the research supports. The breathing most linked to the parasympathetic, rest-and-digest side is slow and gentle: about six breaths per minute, ideally with a longer exhale.

A 2022 meta-analysis of 223 studies found heart rate variability rose during slow breathing, right after one session, and with repeated practice. Big, forceful breaths can do the opposite and leave you more wired.

We wrote a plain-language guide covering which parts of a breath do the work, how Calm 4-6, the physiological sigh, 4-7-8 and box breathing compare, and a 30-second reset for when a feed has wound you up. It's the moment DeRot, our upcoming iOS app, is built for: {url}
